hard_gates.evidence:
No leak found.

API attack (attacks/g1-attack-all.mjs, g1-a-vs-b-r13.txt, g1-run.log): alnoor's admin made 1,981 judged calls against gulfsteel on every OpenAPI route (114 operations, attacks/routes.txt), the new POST /api/identity/users/{id}/devices/approve and /me/devices/approve included. The calls used B's ids in paths and bodies, four tenant-switch headers, tenant query fields, every list export (CSV, XLSX unzipped, PDF; English and Arabic) and the reports with B's role and company ids. Result: 0 leaks, and B's state hash was the same before and after. Gulfsteel's admin approving an alnoor user's device answers 404 (attacks/device-approval.txt).

Database as erp_app (attacks/db-app-role.txt):
- no tenant bound: 0 rows;
- bound to A: 100,005 users and 200,079 audit rows, 0 of B's;
- UPDATE/DELETE of B's rows changed 0 rows, an INSERT for B was refused by RLS;
- row_security=off, SET ROLE erp_owner/postgres/erp_auth and DISABLE RLS were refused;
- all 17 tenant tables have RLS enabled and forced;
- bound to A, writing B's device_approval_hash changed 0 rows, and verify_sign_in answers nothing.

No jobs or files exist yet beyond the report endpoints, which the attack covered.

Walk w1 (screenshots 01 and 02): a user created by keyboard with the Read-only role (Alt+N, e-mail, Alt+O, 'read', Enter, Ctrl+Enter) signs in with the set-up code and chooses a password. They see no New user, Alt+N opens nothing, and their record is read-only with no Save. API calls with their session: POST users 403, POST roles 403.

Escalation (attacks/escalate.txt), by a helpdesk role holding users and roles read/update/create, all refused with 403:
- assign themselves Administrator, or Administrator in one company;
- add permissions to their own role, create a role beyond their own, copy Administrator;
- give others Administrator;
- edit, deactivate, reset, sign out, unblock or delete the Administrator.
New this round: letting a device of the Administrator in is refused 403 (userWorksBeyondOwnCompanies), and on oneself through the admin endpoint 403 (notOnYourself).

Control for U1 below (walks/u1-check-real.txt, screenshot 11): a user who may only read the sign-in history is offered no device form.
